\newcommand{\abstractI}{We contribute the first provable guarantees of global convergence to a Nash equilibrium in two-player zero-sum convex Markov games~\citep{gemp2024convex} (cMGs) through multiple independent learning policy gradient schemes. Convex Markov games have recently been introduced to address a critical gap in modeling multiagent strategic interactions with general convex preferences over occupancy measures. Essentially, cMGs extend convex Markov decision processes to a multi-agent setting. However, this extension introduces significant technical challenges. To overcome them, we provide substantial insights over the properties of hidden-convex--hidden-concave functions and show that after some nontrivial regularization, the underlying min-max optimization problems exhibit a nonconvex--proximal Polyak-\L ojasiewicz (NC-pPL) structure. Crucially, this regularization can stabilize the iterates of independent policy gradient methods and ultimately lead them to converge to equilibria. Building on this insight, we shift our focus to general constrained min–max optimization under NC-pPL and two-sided pPL conditions and present the first global convergence guarantees for nested stochastic gradient iterations and stochastic alternating gradient descent–ascent.}

\newcommand{\abstractII}{\fk{stochatic}We present the first provable guarantees of global convergence to a saddle point for nonconvex functions defined over constrained domains, under one-sided or two-sided proximal Polyak–\L ojasiewicz (pPL) conditions. In particular, we analyze nested stochastic gradient iterations and stochastic alternating gradient descent–ascent in constrained min–max optimization problems under the nonconvex–proximal Polyak–\L ojasiewicz (NC-pPL) and two-sided pPL conditions. Leveraging these results, we establish the first provable convergence guarantees in convex Markov games (cMGs) \citep{gemp2024convex} via multiple independent learning policy gradient methods.

Convex Markov games have recently been introduced to model multiagent strategic interactions with general convex preferences over occupancy measures, thus extending convex Markov decision processes to multiagent settings. However, this extension introduces significant technical challenges. To overcome these challenges, we provide substantial insights over the properties of hidden-convex--hidden-concave functions and show that after some nontrivial regularization, the underlying min-max optimization problems exhibit a nonconvex--proximal Polyak-\L ojasiewicz (NC-pPL) structure. Then, we are able to readily apply our min-max optimization results.
}

