\begin{abstract}
Virtual brain twins are personalized digital models of individual human subject or 
patient's brains, allowing for mechanistic interpretation of neuroimaging data features.
Training \& inference with these models however presents a pair of challenges: (a) large shared
infrastructure do not allow for use of personal data and (b) inference in clinical applications
should not require significant resources.  We introduce "anonymized personalization" to address both by expanding model priors to include
personalization which under amortized inference allows training to be performed anonymously, while
inference is both personalized and lightweight.  
We illustrate the basic approach, demonstrate
reliability in an example, and discuss the impact on both experimental and computational neuroscience. Code is available at \url{https://github.com/ins-amu/apvbt}.
\keywords{Virtual brain twins  \and Amortized inference \and Personalized medicine.}
\end{abstract}
