{\bf The consistency test} We proceed as above. The vector $\bx^*$ is transformed into a $7\times7$ matrix $\bQ^*.$ This matrix is augmented with the first column of data plus the obvious last row to obtain the matrix $\bP^*.$ Then we compute the $15$ powers of $\bP^*$ the last column of each. The first $7$ coincide with the data, and the rest are the predictors of the cumulative defaults probabilities. We then compare to the data provided by S\&P. The comparisons are listed in Table \ref{tab4} below. There we display the differences of the $\ell_1$-norms for the given number of data points and the given number of years into the future. For example cell $(6,12)$ indicates a prediction error of about $5\%$ between the vector of cumulative default probabilities provided by S\&P and the one predicted using our methodology.

