\section{Experimental Design}



To guide the following methodology, we reference the pipeline illustrated in~\Cref{fig:pipeline}.





\subsection{Large Language Models}


Our audit focuses on six open-weight instruction-tuned LLMs: Gemma 2 9B IT~(\texttt{gemma2-9b})~\cite{gemma2_9b_it,gemma_paper}, Mixtral-8x7B-Instruct-v0.1~(\texttt{mixtral-8x7b})~\cite{mixtral8x7b,mixtral_paper}, Meta-Llama-3-8B-Instruct~(\texttt{llama3-8b})~\cite{meta_llama_3_8b}, Meta-Llama-3-70B-Instruct~(\texttt{llama3-70b})~\cite{meta_llama_3_70b}, Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct~(\texttt{llama3.1-8b})~\cite{meta_llama_31_8b}, and Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct~(\texttt{llama3.1-70b})~\cite{meta_llama_31_70b,llama3_paper}.

We selected these models for their open access, instruction tuning, and strong performance across reasoning and language understanding benchmarks. 
\texttt{gemma2-9b}~offers a compact, efficient architecture with a focus on safe deployment. 
\texttt{mixtral-8x7b}~employs a mixture-of-experts design that delivers competitive results while remaining computationally efficient. 
The \texttt{llama 3}~models are strong general-purpose instruction followers with broad adoption. 
The \texttt{llama 3.1}~models extend this capability through improvements in alignment and reasoning, yielding gains in consistency and factual accuracy. 
Together, these models span a wide range of parameter scales, from lightweight $8$B variants to large $70$B models, allowing us to compare performance across different capacities.
Additional details of the models
are available in~\Cref{app:llms}.


We focus on open-weight models to ensure transparency, enable reproducibility, and isolate model-internal biases without interference from proprietary training data, web searches, or hidden model updates~\cite{ollion2024dangers}. This design choice ensures our findings reflect core model behavior while establishing a methodological foundation that can be extended to audit commercial models.



\subsection{Factual Dataset}
To assess the accuracy of the LLMs in each recommendation task, we require a reliable ground-truth on scholars and their contributions. The American Physical Society (APS) dataset~\cite{aps_datasets} provides this foundation, offering complete bibliographic records of physics research since 1893. Physics serves as an ideal domain for examining bias due to its well-documented diversity challenges~\cite{Maries_2025,kong2021first}. We augmented this dataset with metadata from OpenAlex~\cite{priem2022openalexfullyopenindexscholarly} to include broader author-level metrics such as total publications, citations, and $h$-index scores.






