\section{Results}

Each LLM has up to three attempts per request. Responses must include a valid JSON and at least one name; otherwise, they are discarded. If multiple responses are valid, only the first is used for evaluation, see \Cref{app:valid} for details.














\subsection{Consistency}
\label{sec:consistency} 
\Cref{fig:consistency:uniqueness} reports average uniqueness ratios per task, defined as the proportion of distinct names in each recommendation. Models such as \texttt{mixtral-8x7b}~and \texttt{llama3.1-8b}~show the lowest ratios, indicating a tendency to include duplicate names within a single response, particularly in the \textit{field}, \textit{top-k}, and \textit{twins}~tasks.
\Cref{fig:consistency:similarity} shows how often models return identical recommendations across time. \texttt{mixtral-8x7b}~was the most deterministic, repeatedly generating the same outputs, while \texttt{llama3.1-70b}, despite a temperature setting of $t = 0$, showed the highest variability.
Consistency also varies with task parameters. For instance, querying for the top $100$ experts is more likely to result in duplicated names within a single output, see~\Cref{app:consistency} for more details.



