\subsection{Factuality}
\label{sec:factuality}
For each response, we compute the fraction of real scientists across task-specific dimensions. \Cref{fig:factuality} reports averages across valid responses per model and task. We exclude factuality analysis for the \textit{top-k}~and \textit{twins}~tasks, where rankings are inherently subjective. Instead, we provide a descriptive analysis of bias and similarity in~\Cref{sec:bias,sec:similarity}.





\subsubsection{Author factuality.}(\Cref{fig:factuality:author})
Most models accurately retrieve real scientists. While accuracy exceeds $80\%$ in the \textit{top-k}, \textit{field}, and \textit{epoch}~tasks, it declines in the \textit{seniority}~and \textit{twins}~tasks.
%
\texttt{gemma2-9b}~performs worst in the \textit{top-k}~and \textit{field}~tasks, while \texttt{llama3.1-8b}~struggles most with \textit{seniority}. \texttt{llama3.1-70b}~is the most consistent overall, maintaining high accuracy across tasks. \texttt{llama3-8b}~performs well in \textit{top-k}, \textit{epoch}, and \textit{seniority}, whereas \texttt{mixtral-8x7b}~shows high variability and generally low accuracy.
Factuality also varies with task parameters. 
\texttt{mixtral-8x7b}~performs best for influential scientists from the 1950s, while the larger \texttt{llama}~models do better for the 2000s (Appendix~\Cref{app:tbl:factuality_epoch}). Most models perform better when prompted for scientists in CMMP (Appendix~\Cref{app:tbl:factuality_field}) or for senior scholars (Appendix~\Cref{app:tbl:factuality_seniority}).
These results suggest that LLMs are more reliable when recommending well-established scientists, especially those from large or senior-dominated fields. However, hallucinations persist---some names may be fabricated or refer to individuals outside the APS---especially when prompting for top-100 experts, scientists from PER, the 2000s, early-career, or fictional twins (Appendix~\Cref{app:fig:authors:hallucinations}).



\subsubsection*{Field factuality} (\Cref{fig:factuality:field})
In addition to recommending scholars, we asked the LLMs to provide a DOI for a paper authored by each recommended individual. The factuality check then evaluated three conditions: whether the \textit{author} exists, whether the \textit{DOI} exists, and whether both are associated with the requested \textit{field} (A.D.F.).
Among the models, \texttt{gemma2-9b}~performed the worst, followed by \texttt{mixtral-8x7b}. All \texttt{llama}~models performed better, with \texttt{llama3-8b}~achieving the highest accuracy in retrieving real DOIs. However, no model satisfied all conditions simultaneously.
Field-level analysis (Appendix~\Cref{app:tbl:factuality_field}) shows that \texttt{gemma2-9b}~returned no valid responses for CMMP and repeatedly produced the same DOI for all authors in PER. Although this DOI (\texttt{10.1119/1.1991771}) was structurally valid, it did not correspond to a real publication. \texttt{mixtral-8x7b}~differentiated DOIs by field, returning \texttt{10.1103} for CMMP and \texttt{10.1119} for PER, yet all PER references were unrelated to the field and not indexed in APS.
Factuality was consistently higher for CMMP than PER across all models, likely due to the size of each field: 
$86$K authors in CMMP and $1$K in PER 
(Appendix~\Cref{app:fig:gt_demographics_per_field}).
These results suggest that while LLMs perform better in larger, more represented fields, they still struggle to validate and cross-reference multiple constraints reliably.










\subsubsection*{Epoch factuality} (\Cref{fig:factuality:epoch})
In this task, we asked the LLMs to provide the years of activity for each individual. Factuality was then evaluated across five dimensions: whether the \textit{author} exists, whether the author was active during the requested period (Match), and whether the years mentioned by the LLM fell \textit{within} (In$_{(txt)}$), \textit{outside} (Out$_{(txt)}$), or \textit{overlap} with (Over$_{(txt)}$) the specified epoch.
Across models, fewer than 75\% of the recommended scientists were actually active during the requested period. Textual evidence fell within the target range in about half of the responses, and in more than half, it overlapped partially with the correct period. It was rare for the LLMs to return years entirely outside the requested epoch.
%
Among the models, \texttt{gemma2-9b}~performed best at identifying scientists active during the specified period, often providing overlapping evidence. \texttt{llama3-8b}~performed worst in both real matches and supporting evidence.
Factuality trends between epochs (Appendix~\Cref{app:tbl:factuality_epoch}) show that \texttt{mixtral-8x7b}~performed best for the 1950s, while the larger \texttt{llama}~models were more effective for the 2000s. The smaller models (\texttt{llama3-8b}, \texttt{llama3.1-8b}, and \texttt{gemma2-9b}) failed to return valid experts for the 2000s due to technical issues.
These findings highlight limitations in LLMs’ temporal reasoning and suggest caution when using them for time-sensitive academic recommendations.









