\section{Methods}
\label{app:methods}






\subsection{LLMs Description}
\label{app:llms}
In our study, we utilized six different LLMs: \texttt{llama3-8b}, \texttt{llama3.1-8b}, \texttt{gemma2-9b}, \texttt{mixtral-8x7b}, \texttt{llama3-70b}, \texttt{llama3.1-70b}.
These models were selected as they provide a diverse range of parameter sizes and architectures for a comprehensive evaluation. ~\Cref{app:tbl:llm_summary} summarizes key characteristics of each model, including the developer, whether the model is open source, the release date, the training data cut-off, and the total number of parameters. The models are listed in ascending order based on their parameter size, offering insight into their scale and suitability for different computational tasks.

The models' temperature was fixed at $0$ to enforce deterministic outputs, while Groq API\footnote{\url{https://console.groq.com/docs/api-reference#chat-create}}'s default values were retained for other hyperparameters (e.g., $top\_p = 0.1$).


\subsection{Prompts}
\label{app:prompts}
The prompts used for the five tasks in our study are illustrated in~\Cref{app:prompt-topk,app:prompt-field,app:prompt-epoch,app:prompt-seniority,app:prompt-twins,app:system-prompt}. Each prompt corresponds to a specific task designed to evaluate different author search use cases. 
\Cref{app:prompt-topk} displays the prompt for top-k recommendations, which assesses the ability of the LLM to provide short or long lists of names.
Experts by field's prompt, shown in~\Cref{app:prompt-field}, focuses on experts from two different fields with different ratios of female scientists to see whether the LLM recommends more female scientists in the field with higher proportion. 
\Cref{app:prompt-epoch} and \Cref{app:prompt-seniority} provide the prompts for time-based suggestions and seniority-based recommendations, which tests the models’ ability to handle knowledge of current or historical figures.
For statistical twins searches, the prompt in~\Cref{app:prompt-twins} tests the models’ ability to handle knowledge of similar authors.
These prompts collectively provide a robust foundation for evaluating diverse aspects of LLM-based author searches. The \textit{system prompt}, detailed in~\Cref{app:system-prompt}, ensures consistency and accuracy in retrieving relevant physicists.










\subsection{APS dataset}
\label{app:aps}
The APS dataset consists of 678.916 publications spanning from 1893 to 2020, authored by 468.090 unique individuals. Each publication includes metadata such as the year of publication and topic. Additionally, each author has associated metadata within APS, including the number of publications and citations, providing a focused view of physicists' scholarly output. 
To enhance the dataset, we mapped each APS author to their corresponding records in OpenAlex~\cite{priem2022openalexfullyopenindexscholarly}, a large open repository of scholarly data. This augmentation added information such as the authors' total number of publications (across all domains, not limited to APS), total received citations, h-index, and alternative names (i.e., different name variations used by authors in publications). It also includes details on their affiliations and affiliated country.

Furthermore, we inferred the \textit{perceived} gender and \textit{perceived} ethnicity of authors from the their full names using the tools \texttt{demographicx}~\cite{liang2021demographicx}, \texttt{ethnicolr}~\cite{ethnicolr} and \texttt{gender-guesser}~\cite{saeta2016genderguesser}, respectively. These tools achieved $0.84$ and $0.95$ F1-score (details and evaluation metrics can be found in~\Cref{app:inference}). While we acknowledge the limitations of inferring binary gender categories---recognizing that gender is a social construct beyond the binary---we chose binary gender (male and female) as these groups represent the largest populations, ensuring statistical significance in our results. These classifications serve as a preliminary step for measuring potential biases related to gender and ethnicity in the recommended expert sets. In future research, we plan to expand these categories to reflect a more nuanced understanding of both gender and ethnicity. A visualization of gender and ethnicity distributions across APS physics subfields is provided in~\Cref{app:fig:gt_demographics_per_field}. Future work should expand these categories to better reflect global understandings of gender and ethnicity.






