\subsection{Inference}
\label{app:inference}

To gain demographic information about the authors in the APS dataset, we used the tools \texttt{demographicx}~\cite{liang2021demographicx} and \texttt{ethnicolr}~\cite{ethnicolr} to infer ethnicity, and \texttt{gender-guesser}~\cite{saeta2016genderguesser} to infer gender. Note that these inferences do not reflect actual gender identities of people, instead they are perceived characteristics inferred from first and last names. As a means to evaluate the performance of the used tools, a stratified sample of 460 authors was drawn based on the language spoken in the country of the first institution the author is affiliated with. This way, we wanted to best represent all ethnicities within the dataset equally. As a second layer of stratification, we used preliminary gender labels inferred from Google images~\cite{karimi2016inferring} in order to have equal numbers of men and women in the test dataset. Gender and ethnicity of this sample were then evaluated via internet searches by human annotators. Each author was labeled by at least two and at most three annotators. The performance of the tools and their details are explained in the following sections.


\subsubsection{Gender inference.}
\texttt{gender-guesser}~$0.4.0$~\cite{saeta2016genderguesser} predicts the gender of a person based on their first name, using a dictionary based approach encompassing over $40.000$ unique names and additional spelling variations per name. This method accurately identified the correct gender for most cases and was effective at distinguishing between male and female names, with few errors, see~\Cref{app:tbl:gender}. Its ability to make correct predictions was consistently high across both categories, although there were minor variations in performance between men and women. The library also returns an ``andy'' (neutral) classification for androgynous names where the probability of being male or female is approximately equal, distinct from ``unknown'' which indicates names absent from the database.





\subsubsection{Ethnicity inference.}
In order to estimate ethnicity, we combined the tools \texttt{demographicx}~\cite{liang2021demographicx} and \texttt{ethnicolr}~\cite{ethnicolr} to create a fallback model which uses the label retrieved from \texttt{demographicx}~if available, else uses the \texttt{ethnicolr}~label. 
While \texttt{demographicx}~employs a transformer model based on BERT, fine-tuned on the Torvik dataset~\cite{illinoisdatabankIDB-9087546}, \texttt{ethnicolr}~uses character-level recurrent neural networks trained on US census data, Florida voting registration data and Wikipedia data. 
As shown in~\Cref{app:tbl:ethnicity}, the fallback model employed exhibits perfect precision across all ethnicities, however recall varies across them, with the highest performance for Asian names and slightly lower values for Black or African American and Hispanic or Latino groups. This contrast is a result of the tools returning no result if a certainty threshold is not achieved, meaning the model is unable to make a prediction when its confidence in the classification falls below a predefined limit. This means an under-representation of some ethnicities can be assumed. 










\section{Supplementary Results}
The results presented in the main text represent the overall average scores across all tasks and use cases for each model. In this section, we break down these averages to provide detailed scores for each specific use case.




\subsection{Valid Responses}
\label{app:valid}




\subsubsection{Validity of responses} (\Cref{app:fig:validity:fraction})
We measure the percentage of requests per task for which models returned correctly formatted JSON outputs with recommendations. 
\texttt{gemma2-9b}~and \texttt{llama3.1-8b}~performed poorly, with the lowest percentages of valid outputs.
\texttt{gemma2-9b}~frequently refused to retrieve names, a behavior we classified as \textit{invalid}. 
A common issue among models, was the inclusion of verbose text before and/or after the JSON results, see~\Cref{app:prompt-epoch-response}.
In some cases, such as \texttt{mixtral-8x7b}~and \texttt{llama3.1-70b}, the valid JSON could be recovered by removing these \textit{verbose} explanations. 
However, for models like \texttt{llama3-8b}~and \texttt{llama3.1-8b}, responses were often truncated due to token limits, resulting in incomplete JSON formats. 
While these cases could be \textit{fixed}, they frequently contained repeated names.
For subsequent audits, we consider only \textit{valid} and \textit{verbose} responses, treating them interchangeably. 
Under these definitions, only four models achieved high accuracy, consistently producing \textit{valid} responses across tasks. 














\subsubsection{Number of attempts} (\Cref{app:fig:validity:attempts})
To improve response reliability, each LLM was allowed up to two retries per request, resulting in a maximum of three attempts to generate a valid answer.
Thus, we measure the average number of attempts required by each model, along with their standard deviations.
\texttt{llama3.1-8b}~consistently required the maximum number of retries across all tasks, reflecting its difficulties in generating valid responses. On the other hand, \texttt{mixtral-8x7b}~and \texttt{llama3.1-70b}~required the fewest attempts, making them the most reliable models. %
The poor performance of \texttt{gemma2-9b}~can be attributed to its inability to provide valid responses for specific tasks, such as \textit{seniority}~and \textit{twins}. For instance, its responses often included statements like, \textit{``I understand your request. However, I cannot directly access and search real-time information, including publication databases like those of the American Physical Society (APS).''} or \textit{``The provided criteria for identifying scientists are based on the concept of `statistical twins' of Kamala Harris. This is a highly subjective and undefined term.''} 




\subsection{Consistency}
\label{app:consistency}
The consistency of the results returned by the LLMs not only depend on the model and task, but also the parameter passed in the prompt. In~\Cref{app:tbl:uniqueness_within} we see that when the LLMs return valid answers, they all are very likely to retrieve unique names (values close to $0$) when asked for top 5 and CMMP experts, and any expert or influential scientist from the tasks \textit{epoch}~and \textit{seniority}---regardless of whether they are early career, senior, from the 1950s or 2000s. When asked from statistical twins, there is a slight tendency to repeat names when the reference scientist (or person) has a female name, especially for \texttt{llama3.1-8b}~and \texttt{mixtral-8x7b}.
When assessing consistency across multiple requests of the same prompt over time, \Cref{app:tbl:uniqueness_across}, we see that \texttt{gemma2-9b}~and \texttt{mixtral-8x7b}~are the most deterministic (values close to $1$), however, \texttt{gemma2-9b}~is the least reliable as it often does not return any answer. Here, the consistency also varies depending on the parameter of the task. Overall, models tend to return the same set of recommendations when asked for top 5, PER, and 1950s experts. Interestingly, the largest \texttt{llama}~models (\texttt{llama3-70b}, and \texttt{llama3.1-70b}) were more likely to change their recommendations for the same prompt when asked for statistical twins of real people (male or female), while the smallest ones (\texttt{llama3-8b}, and \texttt{llama3.1-8b}) were more consistent.







\subsection{Factuality}
\label{app:factuality}

\subsubsection{Field Factuality.}
\label{app:field}
When an LLM is asked to suggest experts from a specific field, it must provide the full name of the scientist along with the DOI of a paper authored by them in that domain, published by the APS (see prompt in Appendix~\Cref{app:prompt-field}). For each retrieved DOI, we confirm whether the article is indeed authored by the suggested scientist, and ensure that the author is recognized within the relevant field. This validation process is conducted for each retrieved expert across all requests, tasks, and use cases. The aggregated results for each model are presented in Appendix~\Cref{app:tbl:factuality_field}.
























\subsubsection{Epoch Factuality.}
\label{app:epoch}
When an LLM is asked to suggest experts from a specific epoch, it must provide the full name of the scientist along with their active years within the specified period (see prompt in Appendix~\Cref{app:prompt-epoch}). For each retrieved name, we calculate the author's years of activity by extracting distinct publication years, and confirm whether the requested epoch falls within this timeframe. This validation process is applied to each retrieved expert across all requests, tasks, and use cases. The aggregated results for each model are presented in Appendix~\Cref{app:tbl:factuality_epoch}.

























\subsubsection{Seniority Factuality.}
\label{app:seniority}
When an LLM is asked to suggest experts based on specific seniority, it must provide the full name of the scientist along with their estimated career age (see prompt in Appendix~\Cref{app:prompt-seniority}). For each retrieved name, the author's career age is calculated as the difference between their first and last publication years. Using established guidelines for career stages~\cite{milojevic2018changing}, we determine whether the author is classified as early career ($\leq$ 10 years) or senior ($\leq$ 20 years). We then verify if the career age estimated by the LLM matches the actual career age derived from APS records.
This validation process is applied to each retrieved expert across all requests, tasks, and use cases. The aggregated results for each model are presented in Appendix~\Cref{app:tbl:factuality_seniority}.




































































\subsection{Nobel Prize Winners}
\label{app:nobel}
We examine whether LLMs exhibit bias toward Nobel Prize laureates by checking recommended authors against the Nobel laureates dataset~\cite{nobel_laureates_dataset}. While most recommended scholars are not Nobel laureates, the observed proportion exceeds random chance. The baseline probability of randomly selecting a Nobel laureate from the general academic population is approximately $0.00032$, yet our models recommend Nobel laureates at higher rates. \Cref{app:fig:nobel} shows two key patterns. First, as expected given our physics focus, the Nobel laureates recommended are Physics winners, with \texttt{gemma2-9b}~showing the highest proportion at nearly 50\% of its recommendations being Nobel laureates, followed by \texttt{llama3-70b}. Second, rather than recommending laureates evenly across time periods, models show concentrated preferences for specific decades. \texttt{gemma2-9b}~exhibits the strongest temporal clustering with notable peaks in the 1960s and 2000s.
%



\subsection{Popularity bias per model and task}
\label{app:biases}
In academia, metrics such as the number of publications, citations, and h-index are commonly used to measure a scientist's success. For this reason, we ranked all APS authors based on these metrics to generate a distribution, allowing us to compare the percentile ranks of the authors recommended. We observed that LLMs consistently recommend scholars from the upper tiers of academic metrics (~\Cref{app:fig:popularity})s. Across all models, recommended scholars average in the $70^{th}$ percentile or higher for these metrics (see ~\Cref{app:tbl:rank_publication_pct,app:tbl:rank_citation_pct,app:tbl:rank_hindex_pct}). This bias becomes extreme for certain tasks: recommendations for \textit{top-5 experts} and \textit{twins of famous scientists} consistently exceed the $90^{th}$ percentile for both publications and citations across all models (~\Cref{app:tbl:rank_publication_pct,app:tbl:rank_citation_pct}, \textit{Mean} column). The h-index shows similar but somewhat attenuated patterns (~\Cref{app:tbl:rank_hindex_pct}), suggesting LLMs favor established, highly productive researchers. This reflects the well-documented tendency of traditional metrics to favor senior scientists and early movers~\cite{waltman2012inconsistency}, indicating that LLMs reproduce rather than counteract these existing biases.






\subsection{Similarity bias}
\label{app:similarity}

Building on the similarity patterns identified in~\Cref{sec:similarity}, we provide detailed analysis across all tasks and models. 
\Cref{app:fig:similarity} examines four dimensions of similarity among recommended scholars. The career age distribution analysis (\Cref{app:fig:similarity:age}) reveals consistent patterns across tasks, with notably higher Gini coefficients for \textit{top-100} recommendations compared to \textit{top-5} queries across all models, indicating greater age diversity when requesting more experts. 
Scholarly metrics similarity shows generally low scores in both APS (\Cref{app:fig:similarity:aps}) and OpenAlex (\Cref{app:fig:similarity:aps}) metrics. Co-authorship density (\Cref{app:fig:similarity:coauthordensity}) displays mixed patterns, with highest density observed for \textit{top-5} and \textit{twins of famous scientists} recommendations, suggesting models favor highly prominent scientists who are more likely to be interconnected within elite academic networks. \Cref{app:fig:jaccard} provides detailed analysis of network-based similarities through shared affiliations, countries, and co-authors. Affiliation similarity (\Cref{app:fig:jaccard:affiliations}) remains consistently low across most tasks, confirming institutional diversity in recommendations. 
Co-author overlap (\Cref{app:fig:jaccard:coauthors}) is generally low, suggesting that most recommended authors are not within two hops in the collaboration network.

Finally, we conduct Principal Component Analysis (PCA)~\cite{jolliffe2016principal} to visualize how LLM recommendations cluster within the scholarly landscape. We represent each author in our baseline APS population as a normalized 15-dimensional vector comprising complementary metrics from both APS and OpenAlex databases. The metrics include career age (estimated as the span between first and last publication), citations per paper normalized by career age (total citations divided by number of papers and career length to avoid penalizing younger researchers), cited-by count, e-index~\cite{erkol2023consistency}, h-index, i10-index, and publications count. Crucially, these APS metrics capture only citations and collaborations within the physics community network. We supplement these with corresponding OpenAlex metrics covering identical dimensions while reflecting each author's broader academic impact across all disciplines, as well as the two-year mean citedness\footnote{\url{https://docs.openalex.org/api-entities/publishers/publisher-object#summary_stats}} for recent impact assessment. This dual-perspective approach captures both domain-specific physics contributions and cross-disciplinary influence, enabling dimensionality reduction to reveal clustering patterns that remain invisible when examining individual metrics in isolation. 

We find that LLMs appear to differentiate recommendations based on task parameters, but with varying degrees of separation. 
\textbf{Top-k} (\Cref{app:fig:components:topk}): There is a clear distinction between top-5 and top-100 recommendations. Top-5 outputs are tightly clustered near the high-impact region, while top-100 expands modestly outward. This suggests that list size impacts diversity, but the expansion remains constrained.
\textbf{Field }(\Cref{app:fig:components:field}): Recommendations for CMMP and PER show moderate overlap, with some differentiation along PC2. This indicates that models can partially separate fields, but boundaries are fuzzy, possibly reflecting field-related ambiguity in training data or embeddings.
\textbf{Epoch} (\Cref{app:fig:components:epoch}): Recommendations for scholars active in the 1950s vs. the 2000s exhibit more distinct separation. This suggests that models capture temporal shifts in scholarly profiles, with recent scholars spread more widely and older scholars forming a compact cluster. However, the spatial distribution still remains within a narrow PCA region.
\textbf{Seniority} (\Cref{app:fig:components:seniority}): Early-career and senior scholars show partial separation, with senior figures clustering in high-metric regions. This reflects a strong model preference for established researchers, consistent with popularity and citation-based bias.
In all cases, recommended scholars occupy a narrow subregion of the broader APS distribution. The contrast is especially visible along PC1, where the gray shading covers a wide range but LLM outputs cluster tightly around the upper midrange to high end. This indicates that LLMs strongly favor scholars with high productivity and citation profiles. The limited dispersion suggests that models fail to capture the full diversity of the scholarly landscape and reinforce a narrow definition of impact.




When prompting for statistical twins of reference scientists, it becomes crucial to analyze the similarity between recommendations and reference, given the nature of the task to verify also the understanding of the task by each LLM. In~\Cref{app:fig:components_twins} we see that across all models, recommendations are tightly clustered in a narrow region of the PCA space, mostly along the high values of PC1. Larger models produce broader but still focused clusters, while smaller models offer sparse, narrow outputs. The clustering indicates that models favor similar high-impact profiles regardless of size or capacity.
Even when the reference is non-scholarly (e.g., a politician or fictitious name, \Cref{app:fig:components_twins:politic,app:fig:components_twins:tv,app:fig:components_twins:ficticious}), models generate a list of scholars that are not necessarily very similar, indicating limited capacity to reject incoherent prompts.
\textbf{Famous APS scientists} (\Cref{app:fig:components_twins:famous}): Recommended twins are highly clustered near the reference scientist (triangles), reflecting strong similarity in scholarly metrics. The reference often sits at the extreme edge of the PCA space, far from the APS average.
\textbf{Random APS scientists} (\Cref{app:fig:components_twins:random}): Clustering is still present but closer to the center of the PCA distribution, with less extreme positioning of reference scholars. This suggests less emphasis on high-impact profiles.
\textbf{Famous politicians} (\Cref{app:fig:components_twins:politic}): References lie far outside the scholarly PCA space, but recommendations cluster closer to the scholarly distribution, indicating that models revert to plausible but contextually irrelevant scholars. Some scatter is evident, but core clustering remains tight.
\textbf{Famous TV characters} (\Cref{app:fig:components_twins:tv}) and \textbf{fictitious names} (\Cref{app:fig:components_twins:ficticious}): References are non-scholarly and distant from the APS space, yet recommended twins still cluster within the scholarly region, reinforcing the models' default reliance on high-metric scholars. Dispersion increases slightly, but clusters remain narrow compared to the overall population.


















