 This suggests the following question.   
\medskip

\textbf{Question.}
Does the nilpotent graph of $\mathfrak{t}(2,\mathbb{F}_q)$ consist of $q+1$ connected components, and each one of them is $K_{q(q-1)}$?
\bigskip

\noindent \underline{The algebra $L=\mathfrak{t}(2,F_n)$}
\medskip

Put $z=\begin{pmatrix} 1&0\\0&1\end{pmatrix}$, $y=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\0&0\end{pmatrix}$, $F=F_n$. Then $\nil(L)=Z(L)=Fz$, $L^2=Fy$.

\begin{lemma} 
If $x\notin Z(L)$, then $\nil_L(x)$ is a two-dimensional abelian subalgebra of $L$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof} 
Certainly, $\langle x,z\rangle$ is a two-dimensional subalgebra which is contained in $\nil_L(x)\subseteq E_L(x)$. Now $E_L(x)$ is a subalgebra of $L$. If $E_L(x)$ is two-dimensional, then $\nil_L(x)=Fx+Fz$. 
\par

So suppose $E_L(x)=L$. Let $w\in L\setminus \langle x,z\rangle$, so $L=Fz+Fx+Fw$. Then $[x,w]=\lambda y$  and $[x,y]=\mu y$ for some $\lambda, \mu\in F$. Since $w\in E_L(x)$, $\lambda\mu=0$. If $\lambda=0$, then $L$ is abelian, a contradiction, so $\mu=0$. Let $[w,y]=\nu y$. Then $\nu\neq 0$, since, otherwise, $L$ is nilpotent. Hence $\nil_L(x)=Fx+Fz$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
The nilpotent graph of $\mathfrak{t}(2,\mathbb{F}_q)$ consist of $q+1$ connected components, and each one of them is $K_{q(q-1)}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Now $\nil(L)$ is the set of scalar matrices with elements in $F_q$ and there are $q$ of these. Since $L$ has $q^3$ elements, the number of vertices in the graph is $ q^3-q$. If $x\notin Z(L)$, $\nil_L(x)$ is two-dimensional, and so has $q^2$ elements. Thus, the component of the graph containing $x$ has $q^2-q$ vertices; as $\nil_L(x)$ is abelian, that component will be $K_{q(q-1)}$. If $\nil_L(x)\neq \nil_L(y)$ we have that $L=\nil_L(x)+\nil_L(y)$, so $\dim (\nil_L(x)\cap \nil_L(y)=1$, whence  $\nil_L(x)\cap \nil_L(y)=\nil(L)$. Hence, the total number of components in the graph is $(q^3 - q)/(q^2 - q) = q + 1$.
\end{proof}



As a consequence, we have the following.

 \begin{corollary}
The nilpotent graph of $\mathfrak{t}(2,\mathbb{F}_q)$ is $(q^2-q-1)$-regular.     
 \end{corollary}

