	Sideband cooling for any motional mode can inadvertently heat the others~\cite{Chen2017}. Therefore, the three modes were cooled in an alternating and sequential manner. Each cooling cycle for a given motional mode consists of a 5~$\mu$s state-preparation pulse, a 10--30~$\mu$s red-sideband pulse, and a 5~$\mu$s 854-nm pulse to depopulate the $D_{5/2}$ state. As the sideband cooling proceeds, the mean occupation number decreases, and correspondingly, the average Rabi frequency of the red sideband also decreases~\cite{wineland1998}. To sustain a high probability of sideband transition, the durations of the red-sideband pulses are linearly increased in accordance with the number of cooling cycles. In addition, a dark time is inserted between the sideband-cooling and clock-interrogation sequences to characterize the heating rates of the three motional modes due to ambient electric field noise. The clock interrogation can be used to measure the Rabi oscillation of the red sideband or to operate the clock using the hyper-Ramsey excitation. The full timing sequence is depicted in ~Fig.\ref{fig:S3}.

	To evaluate the Doppler cooling performance, we experimentally measured the Rabi oscillations of the carrier for both clock laser beam 1 and beam 3 after Doppler cooling and after sideband cooling, respectively(see Fig.~\ref{fig:S5}). According to the Debye-Waller effect~\cite{wineland1998}, the Rabi frequencies satisfy the following relationships:
	\begin{equation}
		e^{-\left((\eta^1_x)^2 \bar{n}_x + (\eta^1_y)^2 \bar{n}_y + (\eta^1_z)^2 \bar{n}_z\right)} = \frac{\Omega_{\mathrm{Doppler},1}}{\Omega_{\mathrm{SC},1}},
	\end{equation}
	\begin{equation}
		e^{-\left((\eta^3_x)^2 \bar{n}_x + (\eta^3_y)^2 \bar{n}_y\right)} = \frac{\Omega_{\mathrm{Doppler},3}}{\Omega_{\mathrm{SC},3}},
	\end{equation}
	where $\eta^1_i$ ($i=x,y,z$) and $\eta^3_i$ ($i=x,y$) are the Lamb-Dicke parameters of beam 1 and beam 3, respectively, for the corresponding secular motion modes. $\Omega_{\mathrm{Doppler},j}$ and $\Omega_{\mathrm{SC},j}$ ($j=1,3$) represent the Rabi frequencies after sideband cooling, respectively. The experimental results are shown in ~Fig.\ref{fig:S4}. Due to the geometric characteristics of the optical path, $\eta_x \approx \eta_y$, assuming $\bar{n}_x = \bar{n}_y$, we obtained $\bar{n}_x = \bar{n}_y = 2.6$ and $\bar{n}_z = 4.8$. Then, the average temperature was asserted to be $T_{\mathrm{avg}} = 0.76$~mK, which is close to the Doppler cooling limit of 0.52~mK for the $^{40}$Ca$^+$ ion.

