	\subsection{Quadratic Zeeman (AC) shift}
	As indicated in Ref.~\cite{Gan2018}, the quadratic Zeeman shift caused by the component $B_{\parallel}$ of the AC magnetic field $B_{\mathrm{AC}}$ that is parallel to the quantization axis has the same generation mechanism as the quadratic Zeeman shift induced by the DC magnetic field $B_0$. In contrast, the quadratic Zeeman shift arising from the component $B_{\perp}$ of $B_{\mathrm{AC}}$ that is perpendicular to the quantization axis is proportional to the magnetic quantum number $m$. It does not shift the center of a pair of Zeeman transitions but causes a deviation in the measured Zeeman splitting, thereby affecting the measurement of the ratio of the Landé $g$-factor. The specific expression is as follows~\cite{Arnold2019}:
	\begin{equation}\small\label{equation4}
		r=\left[\frac{1+\dfrac{1}{2} \dfrac{\omega_D^2}{\omega_D^2-\Omega_{R F}^2} \dfrac{\left\langle B_{\perp}^2\right\rangle}{B_0^2}}{1+\dfrac{1}{2} \dfrac{\omega_S^2}{\omega_S^2-\Omega_{R F}{ }^2} \dfrac{\left\langle B_{\perp}^2\right\rangle}{B_0^2}}\right] \dfrac{g_{D}}{g_{S}}
	\end{equation}
	where $r$ is the ratio of the Landé $g$-factor between the excited and ground energy levels of the $^{40}\mathrm{Ca}^{+}$ ion clock transition, which is measured through the relationship between two pairs of Zeeman transitions. The ratio $g_D/g_S$ represents the unperturbed value. $\omega_D$ and $\omega_S$ are the Zeeman splittings of the $D_{5/2}$ state and $S_{1/2}$ state, respectively, under a DC magnetic field $B_0$.

	In order to measure the y-direction component of the AC magnetic field, we modulated the DC magnetic field in the $x$-direction with a magnitude of 56.363(5)~$\mu$T. By locking onto two pairs of Zeeman transitions, namely $\left| S_{1/2}, m = \mp 1/2 \right\rangle \rightarrow \left| D_{5/2}, m = \mp 3/2 \right\rangle$ and 
	$\left| S_{1/2}, m = \pm 1/2 \right\rangle \rightarrow \left| D_{5/2}, m = \mp 1/2 \right\rangle$, we measured $r = 0.599\, 4888\, 18(9)$. Then, using the precisely measured value of the ratio $g_D/g_S$ and equation~(\ref{equation4}), we calculated $\langle B_{\perp}^2 \rangle = \langle B_y^2 \rangle + \langle B_z^2 \rangle = 11(9) \times 10^{-14}~\mathrm{T}^2.$ Its uncertainty is mainly limited by the precision of $g_D/g_S$.

	Since the DC magnetic field is in the $y$-direction during the operation of the optical clock, only the $y$-component $B_y$ of the AC magnetic field will cause a quadratic Zeeman shift to the center of a pair of Zeeman transitions. The upper range of $\langle B_y^2 \rangle$ is $1(1) \times 10^{-13}~\mathrm{T}^2$, and the corresponding quadratic Zeeman shift caused by the AC magnetic field induced by the RF drive is $4 \times 10^{-21}$.

	Although higher precision in evaluating the RF-induced AC magnetic field can be achieved by measuring the Autler-Townes splitting and Zeeman precession~\cite{Gan2018,Ma2024}. However, due to constraints from the magnetic shielding structure, it is difficult for the current coil of the LNCIOC $^{40}\mathrm{Ca}^{+}$ system to generate a DC magnetic field that satisfies the resonance condition between the two sublevels of the $S_{1/2}$ state and the RF frequency.



	\subsection{Background gas collisions}

	The collisional frequency shift (CFS), arising from collisions with background helium gas, is calculated using a nonperturbative analytic framework based on a quantum channel description of the scattering process developed by A. C. Vutha et al. This method has been validated through calculations of the fractional frequency shifts for Sr$^{\scriptscriptstyle+}$ and Al$^{\scriptscriptstyle+}$ ions colliding with helium and $\text{H}_{2}$. The expression for the CFS correction is~\cite{Vutha2019}:
	\begin{equation}
		\begin{aligned}
			\label{1}
			\delta\omega &= n_{bg}v \frac{4\pi}{k^2} \sum_{l}(2l+1)(A_{l} + B_{l})\,,\\
			A_{l} &= \frac{1}{4}(\sin2\phi_{l,e} - \sin2\phi_{l,g})\,,\\
			B_{l} &= |\sin\phi_{l,e}\sin\phi_{l,g}|\sin(\phi_{l,e}-\phi_{l,g})\,.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation}
	here, $\phi_{l,g}$ and $\phi_{l,e}$ denote the $l$-th partial wave scattering phase shifts for the ground and excited clock states, respectively. We calculated the phase shifts based on potential energy curves for the Ca$^{\scriptscriptstyle +}$–He system that adiabatically connect to the ground ($S_{1/2}$) and excited ($D_{5/2}$) electronic states. These curves were taken from Ref.~\cite{Guo2020}, where they were computed using the MOLPRO 2010 quantum chemistry package. The calculations employed the multireference configuration interaction (MRCI) method to accurately capture the interaction potentials.

	\begin{figure}[htbp]
		\centering
		\includegraphics[width=9.0cm]{bgs1.jpg}
		\caption{An example of calculated scattering phase shifts at a collision energy 100~K. The phase shift are shown as a function of the partial wave angular momentum, $l$, for collisions of He with Ca$^{+}$ in the ground S and excited D states.}
		\label{bgsfig1}
	\end{figure}


	\begin{figure}[htbp]
		\centering
		\includegraphics[width=9.0cm]{bgs2.jpg}
		\caption{Collision frequency shifts for the Ca$^{+}$ clock transition as a function of collision energy due to helium collisions.}
		\label{bgsfig2}
	\end{figure}

	The scattering wave functions were obtained by numerically integrating the Schr\"{o}dinger equation for each partial wave, with angular momentum values ranging from $l = 0$ to $70$. The partial wave phase shifts for Ca$^{\scriptscriptstyle+}$–He scattering at a collision energy of 100~K are shown in Fig.~\ref{bgsfig1}. These phase shifts were computed using the Ca$^{\scriptscriptstyle+}$–He potential energy curves that adiabatically connect to the ground ($S_{1/2}$) and excited ($D_{5/2}$) clock states. For the excited state, the potential energy curves corresponding to $\Lambda = 0, 1, 2$ were averaged with equal weights to construct an effective $D_{5/2}$ potential. The resulting CFS, as a function of collision energy, is also presented in the Fig.~\ref{bgsfig2}. To obtain the thermally averaged CFS, a Boltzmann-weighted average over collision energies was performed at a temperature of 78.5~K. The final thermally averaged CFS was found to be 42~$\mu\mathrm{Hz}$ at a background gas pressure of $P = 3$~nPa. To evaluate the sensitivity of our result to uncertainties in the interaction potential, we varied the potential depth by $1\%$ and observed a $10\%$ change in $\langle\delta \omega\rangle$ at the same pressure. In an ultra-high-vacuum trapped-ion clock, the dominant residual gases are helium and hydrogen molecules. Because no accurate $ \mathrm{Ca}^+\!-\!\mathrm{H}_2$ potential is available, we estimated the CFS due to $\mathrm{H}_2$ by scaling the known $\mathrm{Ca}^+\!-\!\mathrm{He}$ shift using the long-range $C_4/r^4$ scattering model. In the worst-case limit, the shift scales as $
	\delta\omega \sim n_\text{bg}\,v\,\sigma,$ where $\sigma$ is the elastic cross section for ion-atom collision derived in Ref.~\cite{cote2000}:

	\begin{equation}
		\sigma \;=\;\pi\Bigl(1+\tfrac{\pi^2}{16}\Bigr)\Bigl(\tfrac{\mu\,C_4}{\hbar^2 E}\Bigr)^{1/3}
		\;\propto\;(\mu\,\alpha)^{1/3}.
	\end{equation}


	Here $\mu$ is the reduced mass and $\alpha$ the static polarizability.  Using $\alpha_{\rm He}=1.38\,a_0$~\cite{Mitroy2010} and $\alpha_{\rm H_2}=5.4\,a_0$~\cite{Kolos1967}, we find $
	\frac{\delta\omega_{H_2}}{\delta\omega_{\rm He}} \approx 1.8.
	$
	Thus, at $P = 3$ nPa the CFS due to hydrogen is estimated to be $\langle\delta\omega\rangle = 74\,\mu\mathrm{Hz}$.  For the $\omega_0 = 411$ THz $\mathrm{Ca}^+$ clock transition, this corresponds to a fractional shift
	$
	\frac{\langle\delta\omega\rangle}{\omega_0}
	\approx 1.8 \times 10^{-19}.$ We estimate the upper bound of the collisional frequency shift to be approximately $1.8 \times 10^{-19}$.

