The combined cost-demand graph reveals a critical structural divergence between BTC’s base-layer transaction costs and the Lightning Network’s routing costs. As transaction demand increases, BTC fees escalate logarithmically due to blockspace scarcity and auction-based prioritisation, rapidly pricing out low-value transactions. In contrast, LN routing costs flatten asymptotically, reflecting efficient liquidity reuse and decentralised routing with bounded marginal expense. When combined, the total cost curve initially grows modestly but bends upward as BTC’s cost dominance accelerates beyond moderate demand levels. This creates a nonlinear inflection where the viability of small-value payments becomes contingent on successful off-chain scaling. The graph thus encapsulates the asymptotic constraint of BTC’s design and underscores LN’s role in preserving transactional accessibility under rising throughput pressures.














