\begin{abstract}
% Collaborative group work in classrooms requires teachers to track each student's progress in group tasks, but the sheer number of small groups makes it difficult for teachers to know when to intervene. This highlights the need for AI-driven systems that can monitor group interactions, analyze collaboration dynamics, and alert teachers when intervention is necessary.  

Collaborative Problem-Solving (CPS) markers capture key aspects of effective teamwork, such as staying on task, avoiding interruptions, and generating constructive ideas. An AI system that reliably detects these markers could help teachers identify when a group is struggling or demonstrating productive collaboration. Such a system requires an automated pipeline composed of multiple components.
%To achieve fully automated CPS detection, a robust processing pipeline is required, with several key decisions influencing its design. 
In this work, we evaluate how CPS detection is impacted by automating two critical components: transcription and speech segmentation. On the public Weights Task Dataset (WTD), we find CPS detection performance with automated transcription and segmentation methods is comparable to human-segmented and manually transcribed data; however, we find the automated segmentation methods reduces the number of utterances by 26.5\%, impacting the the granularity of the data. We discuss the implications for developing AI-driven tools that support collaborative learning in classrooms.


\end{abstract}

