For $\percentageuniquetraces$, consider the following event logs:
\begin{align*}
	L_1 &= [\langle a \rangle, \langle a,b,c \rangle^{5}] \\
	L_2 &= L_1 + [\langle a,a,b,b,c,c,d,d \rangle] \\
	L_3 &= L_2 + [\langle a,a,b,b,c,c,d,d \rangle, \langle f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p,q \rangle]
\end{align*}
These event logs are the same as before, but the frequency of the trace $\langle a,b,c \rangle$ increased.
Thus, the directly follows models for these logs are the same as those in \cref{fig:dfm-sequentiality}. 
But these logs have an increasing percentage of unique traces, i.e., $\percentageuniquetraces(L_1) \approx 0.3333 < \percentageuniquetraces(L_2) \approx 0.4286 < \percentageuniquetraces(L_3) \approx 0.4444$.
Thus, we have $(\mathcal{C}^L, \sequentiality) \in \norel$ for all $\mathcal{C}^L \in \loc$. \hfill$\square$
